    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The edicts are the earliest written and datable texts from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and because they were cut in stone they survive as they were first inscribed, unlike the Vedic texts, which were composed and handed down orally.</li><li>They set out a policy of dhamma, an ethic of non-violence, tolerance of all sects, obedience to parents and humane treatment of servants, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romila-thapar">Romila Thapar</a> describes as one to which no religious or social group could object.</li><li>The Brahmi and Kharoshthi scripts of the edicts had become extinct by about the 5th century CE, and were deciphered in the 1830s by Christian Lassen and James Prinsep.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In Minor Rock Edict No. 1 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a> says he had been on tour for 256 days, and the numeral 6 in that number is counted among the earliest ancestors of the digit used today.</p></aside>
    </section>

