    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 260 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandahar">Kandahar</a> Bilingual Rock Inscription, in Greek and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a>, is written in the 10th year of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a>'s reign at the border of his empire with the Hellenistic world</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">256 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The Minor Pillar Edicts begin, from the 12th year of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a>'s reign</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 256 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Major Rock Edict No. 13, which names Hellenistic kings, is dated to about the 14th year of the reign</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">237 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The Major Pillar Edicts are inscribed from the 26th and 27th years of the reign, the most technically elegant of the inscriptions</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 400</span><span class="rf-text">The Chinese traveller Fa Hian refers to one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a>'s pillar edicts at Ni-li, an edict that has not been found</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1836</span><span class="rf-text">Christian Lassen makes the first successful attempt to decipher Brahmi, using the bilingual Greek-Brahmi coins of Agathocles</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1838</span><span class="rf-text">James Prinsep publishes translations of a large number of rock edicts, after identifying the rest of the Brahmi characters</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1915</span><span class="rf-text">An inscription found at Maski confirms that the title Devanampiya belongs to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ai-khanoum"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ai-khanoum.jpg" alt="Ai-Khanoum" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ai-khanoum">Ai-Khanoum</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AiKhanumCapitals.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Artacoana, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The edicts are attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a> of the Mauryan Empire, who ruled most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a> from 268 BCE to 232 BCE. Another reckoning places his reign at 269 to 233 BCE. By the edicts' own account, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinga">Kalinga</a> conquest of the eighth year of his reign was the starting point for his remorse over the horrors of war and for his gradual conversion to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>. The edicts name the Hellenistic kings who were his neighbours, and the Greek communities of the northwest, left after the conquests of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> around 323 BCE, were still significant in his time.</p><p>Earlier Indian texts, such as the Vedic texts, were composed and handed down orally. Ashoka was probably the first Indian ruler to create stone inscriptions. Some scholars see Achaemenid influence on the edicts: the word lipi, which Ashoka used for his inscriptions, is thought to come from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-persian">Old Persian</a> dipî, used by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a> in the Behistun inscription, and the act of engraving edicts on rock may follow Achaemenid practice. It has also been suggested that the Delphic maxims inscribed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ai-khanoum">Ai-Khanoum</a> around 300 BCE, which resemble the edicts in wording and content, may have influenced them.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="content">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What It Is</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ashoka"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ashoka.jpg" alt="Ashoka" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ashoka%27s_visit_to_the_Ramagrama_stupa_Sanchi_Stupa_1_Southern_gateway.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Photo Dharma from Sadao, Thailand, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The edicts fall into four categories by size and medium: Minor Rock Edicts, Minor Pillar Edicts, 14 Major Rock Edicts with 2 separate ones in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a>, and 7 Major Pillar Edicts. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a> called them Dhaṃma Lipi, the inscriptions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharma">Dharma</a>. The Minor Rock Edicts and Minor Pillar Edicts are very religious: they mention the Buddha, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sangha">Sangha</a> and Buddhist scriptures, and Minor Rock Edict No. 3, found beside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bairat">Bairat</a> Temple, lists sermons that monks, nuns and laypeople were to hear and meditate on. The Major Rock Edicts and Major Pillar Edicts never mention the Buddha. They are moral and political, concerned with order, proper behaviour, non-violence, the administration of the state and relations with foreign countries.</p><p>The dhamma they promote is described as largely ecumenical and non-sectarian. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romila-thapar">Romila Thapar</a> lists its principles as non-violence, tolerance of all sects, obedience to parents, respect to brahmins and other religious teachers, liberality toward friends, humane treatment of servants and generosity towards all. Major Rock Edict No. 1 says that animals killed daily in the royal kitchen had fallen from many hundred thousands to three. Major Rock Edict No. 2 records medical treatment for men and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, and Major Rock Edict No. 12 says that those who praise their own sect and blame others injure their own sect severely. Major Rock Edict No. 13 names Antiochos, Ptolemy, Antigonos, Magas and Alexander as kings reached by the Dharma.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Edicts of Ashoka&#8221; cites where it covers Content of the Edicts, Empire boundaries, Moral precepts, Religious precepts:</p><ul><li>Eugen Hultzsch, Inscriptions of Asoka. New Edition by E. Hultzsch (Clarendon Press, 1925)</li><li>Nayanjot Lahiri, Ashoka in Ancient India (Harvard University Press, 2015)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="making">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Was Made</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sanchi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sanchi.jpg" alt="Sanchi" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sanchi">Sanchi</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stupa_1,_Sanchi_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bernard Gagnon, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Three languages were used: Ashokan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prakrit">Prakrit</a>, Greek and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a>, the last an official language of the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>. The Prakrit varied from early Gandhari in the northwest to Old Ardhamagadhi in the east, where it served as the chancery language of the court, and its register tends to be informal or colloquial. Prakrit was written in Brahmi and, in the area of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, Kharoshthi. The Greek of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandahar">Kandahar</a> Greek Edict is of a very high level and shows philosophical refinement. In the south, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andhra-pradesh">Andhra Pradesh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a> used the Prakrit of the north and not the local Dravidian language, which has been interpreted as a kind of authoritarianism towards the newly conquered territories.</p><p>The inscriptions were located in public places and were meant for people to read. The Major Rock Edicts stand not in the Mauryan heartland, traditionally centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a>, but on the frontiers. The earliest edicts are the Minor Rock Edicts, whose engraving is generally very poor; the Major Pillar Edicts of the 26th and 27th years are the most technically elegant. According to Irwin, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarnath">Sarnath</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanchi">Sanchi</a> pillar inscriptions were cut by inexperienced Indian engravers when stone engraving was still new in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, while the Sarnath capital was made under craftsmen from the former Achaemenid Empire.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Edicts of Ashoka&#8221; cites where it covers Questions of authorship:</p><ul><li>Christopher I. Beckwith, Greek Buddha: Pyrrho's Encounter with Early Buddhism in Central Asia (Princeton University Press, 2015)</li><li>Patrick Olivelle, Ashoka: Portrait of a Philosopher King (Yale University Press, 2024)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="reception">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Reception</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmi-script">Brahmi script</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prakrit">Prakrit</a> of the edicts remained in popular use through the Kushan period and could be read down to the 4th century CE, under the Gupta rulers. The edicts were thus there for everyone to see and understand for nearly 700 years, which suggests they remained influential for a long time. Their Prakrit is thought to have given Prakrit great prestige, so that it remained the main inscriptional language for centuries, until inscriptional <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> rose from the 1st century CE. Around 400, the Chinese traveller Fa Hian wrote of an edict of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a> at Ni-li, which has not yet been found.</p><p>The edicts also state that Ashoka sent emissaries and medicinal plants to the Hellenistic kings. It is not clear from Hellenic records whether these emissaries were received or had any influence. Colonial-era scholars such as Rhys Davids attributed Ashoka's claims of conquest by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharma">Dharma</a> to vanity and doubted that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> could have been influenced by Indian thought. Other authors have noted parallels between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, Cyrenaicism and Epicureanism, and some suggest that Hegesias of Cyrene may have been influenced by Ashoka's missionaries. Louis Robert suggests that the emissaries to the western courts were Greek citizens of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandahar">Kandahar</a>. André Dupont-Sommer proposed that Ashoka's missions influenced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/essenes">Essenes</a> and Therapeutae. The evidence for Buddhists in the west at that time is very meagre.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Edicts of Ashoka&#8221; cites where it covers Achaemenid inscriptional tradition, Hellenistic inscriptions, Ancestor of the Hindu–Arabic numeral system, Influence on the Indian epigraphy:</p><ul><li>Richard Salomon, Indian Epigraphy: A Guide to the Study of Inscriptions in Sanskrit, Prakrit, and the other Indo-Aryan Languages (Oxford University Press, 1998)</li><li>Eugen Hultzsch, Inscriptions of Asoka. New Edition by E. Hultzsch (Clarendon Press, 1925)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="survival">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Survival &amp; Rediscovery</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Brahmi and Kharoshthi scripts both became extinct around the 5th century CE and were undeciphered when the edicts were discovered and investigated in the 19th century. Christian Lassen made the first successful attempts at Brahmi in 1836, using the bilingual Greek-Brahmi coins of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greek</a> king Agathocles. James Prinsep, an official of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>, identified the rest of the characters with the help of Major Cunningham and published his results in March 1838. Richard Salomon calls his rendering of the Brahmi alphabet virtually perfect. Prinsep deciphered Kharoshthi in parallel with Lassen, using bilingual Greek-Kharoshthi coinage. The Greek and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> inscriptions were discovered only in the 20th century.</p><p>Prinsep first assumed that the King Devanampriya Piyadasi of the edicts was a Sri Lankan king, and then linked the title to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a> through Pali material from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a> communicated by George Turnour. An inscription found in 1915 by C. Beadon at Maski confirmed the identification, and another at Gujarra gives Devanampiya Piyadasi Asokaraja. Christopher I. Beckwith proposed that Piyadasi, author of the major edicts, and Ashoka, named in the minor ones, were different rulers; his theories are not accepted by mainstream scholarship. Patrick Olivelle calls the theory an outlier that no mainstream Ashokan scholar would subscribe to.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/lumbini"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lumbini.jpg" alt="Lumbini" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/lumbini">Lumbini</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BRP_Lumbini_Mayadevi_temple.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bibek Raj Pandeya, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The edicts provide the first tangible evidence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>. The Rummindei Edict records <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a>'s visit to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lumbini">Lumbini</a> and names it as the birthplace of the Buddha, and uses for the first time in historical records the epithet Sakyamuni. Ashoka was probably the first Indian ruler to create stone inscriptions, and in doing so began an important Indian tradition of royal epigraphical inscriptions. The earliest known stone inscriptions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> date from the 1st century BCE.</p><p>The first examples of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-arabic-numeral-system">Hindu–Arabic numeral system</a> appear in the Brahmi numerals of the edicts. The system was not yet positional: the zero and a mature positional system came around the 6th century CE. The modern glyph for 6 evolved in a rather straightforward way from the numeral in Minor Rock Edict No. 1. Scholars also note that the edicts reflect the desire of rulers more than actual events: a fine of 100 panas for poaching deer in imperial hunting preserves shows that rule-breakers existed. The wildlife provisions have led some to call Ashoka perhaps the first ruler in history to advocate conservation measures. Thapar suggests that the dhamma could also act as a focus of loyalty to weld together the diverse strands of the empire.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Edicts of Ashoka are more than thirty inscriptions on pillars, boulders and cave walls, attributed to the Mauryan emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a>. They set out his policy of dhamma and are the earliest written and datable texts from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. James Prinsep deciphered their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmi-script">Brahmi script</a> in 1838.</em></p>

